            {
              "text": "For wills, estates, and estate litigation in Brisbane East and the Bayside—specifically around **Carindale, Wynnum, and neighbouring suburbs**—several law firms have physical offices, long-standing local ties, or dedicated mobile practices covering **Family Provision Applications (FPAs)**, **will contests**, and **estate disputes**:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Locally Based Firms in Wynnum & Carindale\n\n*   **Connor Hunter Lawyers (Wynnum & Bayside)**\n    *   **Focus:** A long-standing firm in Wynnum (originating in 1971). They have a dedicated **Wills & Estates and Estate Litigation** practice.\n    *   **Matters handled:** Contested wills, defending executors against claims, Family Provision Applications under the *Succession Act 1981* (Qld), and broader estate administration disputes.\n    *   **Location:** Wynnum (and Cleveland).\n\n*   **Fulljames Law (Carindale)**\n    *   **Focus:** A boutique succession and estates law firm based in Carindale. \n    *   **Matters handled:** Specifically advertises and handles **Family Provision Applications**, deceased estate challenges, executor disputes, and estate litigation.\n    *   **Location:** Carindale.\n\n*   **Willwise (Carindale)**\n    *   **Focus:** Led by an accredited specialist in succession law with decades of experience.\n    *   **Matters handled:** Primarily specialised in complex probate, administration of contentious or messy estates, and resolving estate distributions.\n    *   **Location:** Carindale.\n\n*   **JDS Lawyers (Wynnum & Brisbane)**\n    *   **Focus:** Boutique practice with a strong presence in the Wynnum/Bayside area.\n    *   **Matters handled:** Estate litigation, including concerns regarding will validity (lack of capacity or undue influence), mediation between beneficiaries, and family provision claims.\n    *   **Location:** Servicing Wynnum / Bayside.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Immediate Bayside & Inner-East Bordering Firms\n\n*   **Bickell & Mackenzie (Bayside / Redland Bay)**\n    *   **Focus:** Long-established Bayside law firm with strong local ties.\n    *   **Matters handled:** Disputed estates, contesting unfair wills, defending estates against incoming claims, and negotiating FPA settlements.\n    *   **Location:** Redland Bay / servicing the wider Bayside.\n\n*   **Bennett Carroll Solicitors (Upper Mount Gravatt / Brisbane East)**\n    *   **Focus:** Located just minutes west of Carindale.\n    *   **Matters handled:** Extensive experience handling contested wills, claims by excluded children or spouses, and representation in the Supreme Court of Queensland for estate litigation.\n    *   **Location:** Upper Mount Gravatt.\n\n*   **Kate Redman & Associates (Mobile / Bayside & Carindale Service)**\n    *   **Focus:** A mobile wills and estates practice operating across Brisbane East, Carindale, and the Bayside.\n    *   **Matters handled:** Pure estate planning, deceased estates, and contested estates/family provision claims.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Dedicated Estate Litigation Firms Servicing the Area\n\nFor complex Supreme Court litigation—or if seeking **conditional fee arrangements (\"No Win, No Fee\")**—clients in Carindale and Wynnum also frequently instruct dedicated CBD and regional estate litigation boutiques that service the eastern suburbs:\n\n*   **The Estate Lawyers:** One of Queensland’s few firms dedicated solely to estate litigation, will disputes, and defending estates.\n*   **Attwood Marshall Lawyers:** Dedicated estate litigation department handling Family Provision Applications, executor removal, and testamentary capacity challenges across South-East Queensland.\n*   **Queensland Estate Lawyers (QEL):** Practice specialising in will disputes and family provision claims, frequently offering no-win-no-fee arrangements for strong estate claims.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Queensland Estate Litigation Deadlines\n\nIf contemplating a claim against a deceased estate in Queensland under the *Succession Act 1981* (Qld), remember the statutory deadlines:\n1. **6 Months:** The executor must receive formal written notice of your intention to make a Family Provision claim within **6 months** of the date of death.\n2. **9 Months:** The originating application must be filed in Court (Supreme or District Court) within **9 months** of the date of death.",
